# the x86-64 register file: the general registers at each width, the vector, opmask, x87, mmx and amx state, the segment,
# control and debug registers, the flags and the instruction pointer, with their dwarf and codeview numbers
#
# sources, read as published:
#   Intel SDM 325462-093US (September 2026)
#     Vol. 1 3.4.1.1: in 64-bit mode a 32-bit write is zero-extended to the 64-bit register and an 8 or 16-bit write leaves the rest
#     Vol. 1 3.4.2, 3.4.3, 3.4.3.4, 3.5: the segment registers, the flags and their bit positions, rflags, the instruction pointer
#     Vol. 1 8.1.2, 8.1.3, 8.1.5, 10.2.3: the x87 data registers, status and control words, mxcsr
#     Vol. 1 9.2.2, 9.6.2: mmx registers and their aliasing of the x87 registers
#     Vol. 1 14.8, 15.1.1 to 15.1.3: a legacy sse write keeps bits above 127, a vex.128 write zeroes the rest of the register, the
#       zmm, ymm and xmm views, the opmask registers at MAX_KL of 64 bits
#     Vol. 2B MOVDDUP: the same for the evex forms, and for vex.256 and evex.256 above bit 255
#     Vol. 1 19.1: the tile registers, 16 rows of 64 bytes at most, and tilecfg
#     Vol. 2A Table 3-1 and Vol. 2 Table B-8: the hardware numbers of the general and segment registers
#     Vol. 2B MOV to and from the control and debug registers: cr0 to cr7 and cr8, dr0 to dr7
#   Intel APX Architecture Specification 355828-009US (revision 9.0)
#     3.1.2.3.1 and 3.1.2.2: r16 to r31, and the zeroing of bits 63 and above OSIZE only by a new data destination or zu form
#   x86-64 psABI e1ce098331da5dbd66e1ffc74162380bcc213236 (master): low-level-sys-info.tex, "DWARF Register Number Mapping"
#   llvm-project db9b595ae3b30d67bd4068f80c651f97eb4dea2d
#     llvm/include/llvm/DebugInfo/CodeView/CodeViewRegisters.def: the codeview numbers, which match cvconst.h
#
# the numbers are read from those sources, never typed, and agree with what llvm-mc writes for .cfi_offset of each register it
# knows and with the codeview ids clang writes for its register-resident locals. the psABI numbers tmm0 to tmm7, tilecfg,
# mxcsr, fcw and fsw, which llvm does not, and the psABI is the source for those
#
# a register of one width is a row of its own class, as the class holds one width, and the views of one physical register share
# its bank: al, ax, eax and rax are rows of bank 0, ah is bank 0 at bit 8, and xmm0, ymm0 and zmm0 are rows of bank 32. the
# legacy high bytes are a class of their own, as they and spl, bpl, sil and dil share the numbers 4 to 7 (Vol. 1 3.4.1.1)
#
# the x87 registers are named relative to the stack top, so an st register may be any physical register and an mm register is
# one physical register. every st row therefore overlaps every mm row and every other st row, the only footprint that holds at
# every stack top. the pieces of an st row are staggered so that none lies within another row: overlap says what may share
# storage, and a row that lay within another would claim that a write kills it. the mm rows are one physical register each
# and overlap nothing but the st rows
#
# the file has one scale, xlen, the width of the 64-bit registers cr, dr, rflags and rip, which the 64-bit mode fixes at 2.
# 32-bit x86 fixes it at 1 once the catalog has the mode

