# a write through eax, r8d to r15d and r16d to r31d zero-extends to the 64-bit register, and every other write of a general
# register keeps the rest (Intel SDM Vol. 1 3.4.1.1). an Intel APX new data destination or zu form zeroes the rest of an 8 or
# 16-bit write as well (Intel APX 3.1.2.3.1), which is the encoding's to state on its operand. a legacy sse write of xmm0 to
# xmm15 keeps bits above 127 and a vex or evex write zeroes them up to the widest register (Intel SDM Vol. 1 14.8), so those
# rows keep and the encoding states the zeroing. xmm16 to xmm31 are written by evex only and ymm by vex or evex only, and
# every such write at 128 or 256 bits zeroes the rest of the register (Intel SDM Vol. 2B MOVDDUP, the vex.128, evex.128,
# vex.256 and evex.256 forms), so those rows zero. a write of an mmx register sets bits 64 to 79 of its x87 register (Intel SDM
# Vol. 1 9.6.2), which no row states: the fact reaches one register of the shared x87 bank and a row's extension reaches the
# whole bank, so it is left to be stated and the mm rows keep
val REGISTERS: [305]register.Register = [305]register.Register{
    register.Register{name: "rax", class: 0, number: 0, bank: 0, offset: register.Scaled{fixed: 0, per: 0, scale: 0}, stride: register.Scaled{fixed: 0, per: 0, scale: 0}, count: register.Scaled{fixed: 1, per: 0, scale: 0}, flags: 0, requires: requirement.Requirement{at: ?ALT_X64[0], len: 1}, extend: register.KEEP},
    register.Register{name: "rcx", class: 0, number: 1, bank: 1, offset: register.Scaled{fixed: 0, per: 0, scale: 0}, stride: register.Scaled{fixed: 0, per: 0, scale: 0}, count: register.Scaled{fixed: 1, per: 0, scale: 0}, flags: 0, requires: requirement.Requirement{at: ?ALT_X64[0], len: 1}, extend: register.KEEP},
